Title: "Subsets"
Id: "d1d71d30-1ce6-427e-bbc4-dd3ccf53fe53"


Text: "A fundamental way to compare two mathematical sets is through the relation
       of *inclusion* or *subsethood*. We say that a set $A$ is a subset of $B$,
       denoted $A \subseteq B$, when every element belonging to $A$ also belongs
       to $B$.

       It is essential to distinguish between the primitive relation of
       membership ($x \in A$) and the derivative relation of inclusion ($A
       \subseteq B$). Membership relates an individual entity to an enclosing
       collection and is non-transitive: $x \in y$ and $y \in z$ do not imply $x
       \in z$. Inclusion, by contrast, is a relationship between sets defined
       universally: it is reflexive ($A \subseteq A$) and transitive ($A
       \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$ imply $A \subseteq C$)."
Id: "7bfbdad3-bf51-4e2d-ae06-8dd2fa3a220d"


[A \:subset:/ B]
Declares: A is \set
when: B is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: X "in" A
  then: X "in"? B
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" A :-> x_ "in" B
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is a subset of $B?$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "$A \subseteq B$ holds when every element of $A$ is also an
                element of $B$. By extensionality, two sets are equal exactly
                when each is a subset of the other."
Id: "2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e"


Text: "When $A$ is contained in $B$ but the two sets are not identical, we say
       that $A$ is a *proper subset* of $B$, denoted $A \subsetneq B$. This
       requires two independent conditions: every element of $A$ must belong to
       $B$, and $B$ must contain at least one element $b$ such that $b \notin
       A$. Proper inclusion constitutes a strict partial order on collections
       of sets."
Id: "c27fbf42-311e-4efd-b4c8-2da3ad6370a7"


[A \:(proper)::subset:/ B]
Refines: A
when: B is \set
specifies: A \:subset:/ B
satisfies: A != B
Documented:
. adjective: "proper"
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "$A \subsetneq B$ holds when $A$ is a subset of $B$ but is not
                equal to $B$; that is, $B$ has at least one element not in $A$."
Id: "7d070f37-61a5-4c62-b8da-4831c222e4b2"


Text: "Subset inclusion satisfies the transitivity property: if one collection
       is nested inside a second, and that second is nested inside a third, then
       the first is contained within the third. This elementary result is the
       first formal theorem of the collection."
Id: "370b0f76-ab0f-4f7f-80fa-120bb7ffe9e2"


Theorem:
given:
. C is \set
. B \:subset:/ C
. A \:subset:/ B
then:
. A \:subset?:/ C
Documented:
. called: "Transitivity of Subset Relation"
. description: "If $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$, then $A \subseteq C$."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$. Let $x \in A$ be an
          arbitrary element. By definition of $A \subseteq B$, the condition
          $x \in A$ implies $x \in B$. Since $x \in B$, the condition
          $B \subseteq C$ similarly implies $x \in C$. Because $x \in A$ was
          arbitrary, every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, which establishes
          $A \subseteq C$."""
Id: "6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720"


Text: "The characterization of set equality by mutual inclusion—often called the
       *double containment* principle—is one of the most powerful and heavily
       employed proof strategies in all of mathematics. To demonstrate that two
       independently defined sets $A$ and $B$ are identical, one routinely
       proves the two directions $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq A$ as separate
       lemmas."
Id: "a0b6d62f-1f1d-4be5-997b-40aa03cc4ac7"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: A = B
iff:
. A \:subset?:/ B
. B \:subset?:/ A
Documented:
. called: "Set Equality via Mutual Inclusion"
. description: "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are equal if and only if $A \subseteq B$
                and $B \subseteq A$."
Proof: """By the Axiom of Extensionality, $A = B$ if and only if for all $x$,
          $x \in A \iff x \in B$. Decomposing this universal biconditional into
          its two conditional directions yields
          $(\forall x, x \in A \implies x \in B) \land (\forall x, x \in B \implies x \in A)$.
          The first conjunct is precisely the definition of $A \subseteq B$, and
          the second conjunct is the definition of $B \subseteq A$."""
Id: "d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100"


SectionTitle: "The Inclusion Order"
Id: "2c6cb7fb-49cd-41a9-8ce3-c143e7cc045c"


Text: "When we restrict our attention to the collection of all subsets of a
       fixed ambient set $U$—the power set $\mathcal{P}(U)$—the subset relation
       imposes a rich structural architecture. Inclusion is reflexive,
       antisymmetric, and transitive, endowing $\mathcal{P}(U)$ with the
       structure of a partially ordered set (poset). In this order, the empty
       set $\emptyset$ serves as the unique global minimum (since $\emptyset
       \subseteq A$ for every $A$), and the ambient set $U$ serves as the unique
       global maximum (since $A \subseteq U$ for every $A$)."
Id: "d74deced-879a-4cb2-8af3-d10013469765"


[\inclusion.order:on{U}]
Defines: R is \partial.order:on{\power.set:of{U}}
when: U is \set
expresses:
. forAll: A, B "in" \power.set:of{U}
  then:
  . have: R(A, B)
    iff: A \:subset?:/ B
. exists: E "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . E = \empty.set
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(E, A)
. exists: T "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . T = U
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(A, T)
Documented:
. called: "inclusion order on the power set of $U?$"
. description: "Subset inclusion is a partial order on $\mathcal{P}(U)$, with
                the empty set as its least element and $U$ as its greatest
                element."
Id: "ff6d7e2f-1927-4d30-b771-34ea8726711c"


[\family.of.subsets:of{U}]
Declares: A \:subset:/ \power.set:of{U}
when: U is \set
Documented:
. called: "family of subsets of $U?$"
. description: "A family of subsets of $U$ is a subset
                $\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{P}(U)$. Thus $x\in A$ concerns an
                element of one subset, while $A\in\mathcal{A}$ concerns one set
                in the family."
Id: "df5630a9-0ac4-4920-995a-7002bdccbf15"
